<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Anglo-Sikh War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a>–<a href="/years/1846">1846</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> War was fought in 1845–1846 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> and the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> army fought hard but was defeated, partly owing to the manoeuvres of its own leaders. The peace treaty ceded territory, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, and placed the Sikh state under British influence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war.jpg" alt="First Anglo-Sikh War" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Anglo-Sikh_War_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b> (1845–1846) ended in Sikh defeat at Sobraon; the Treaty of Lahore stripped the empire of the Jullundur Doab and Kashmir, which the British then sold to the Dogra Gulab Singh for 7.5 million rupees under the Treaty of Amritsar (1846).</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b> in 1846 the British confirmed the Rajas in their state.</blockquote><a href="/state/chamba-state">Chamba State</a></li><li><blockquote>Raja Pahar Singh sided with the British in the <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b> in 1845 and was rewarded with land.</blockquote><a href="/state/faridkot-state">Faridkot State</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b>, the British sold him the Kashmir valley by the Treaty of Amritsar in 1846, creating a large state under the Dogra dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">Jammu and Kashmir (princely state)</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b> it passed to the British in 1846 and became part of Kangra district; the royal family kept only the jagir of Rupi.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kullu">Kingdom of Kullu</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b> of 1845–46 Ajit Singh joined the Sikh army and took part in the campaign around Buddowal and Aliwal.</blockquote><a href="/state/ladwa-state">Ladwa State</a></li></ul></section>
